From: logdog2325 <loganjayfisher@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 21:00:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Android: PBR team editor

Building a PBR team on Android meant moving PbrSaveData to a PC, opening
PKHeX, and moving it back. This adds a team editor for the game's own NAND
save, as a sibling of the XD-side Gen 3 editor: same package, same Sea
styling, same Showdown import, same verified-write discipline.

The save format
---------------
PbrSaveData is 0x380000 bytes = two 0x1C0000 partitions, each independently
encrypted with the Genius Sonority stream cipher and covered by two 16 x u32
big-endian checksum blocks (a bit-population count, not a sum). Each partition
holds four 0x6FF00 profiles, and each profile a 6-slot party and 18 boxes of
30 packed 136-byte BK4 records. The live partition is chosen by save counter
among the partitions whose checksums verify.

This was derived from PKHeX (kwsch/PKHeX @ 757a297f1463) and proven against a
real 3.5 MB save before a line of Kotlin was written; the Kotlin is a port of
that proven reference, not a re-derivation. Three things in BK4 differ from
the better-known PK4, and each one silently produces garbage if missed:

  * there is NO XOR cipher at rest -- decoding is only a block un-shuffle
  * TID and SID are swapped: SID at 0x0C, TID at 0x0E
  * the IV/flag u32 bit layout is mirrored

Everything multi-byte is big-endian, so the reads and writes go through
PbrBytes, whose function names carry the endianness (readU16BE / writeU32BE).
No type punning, no reuse of the Gen 3 little-endian helpers.

Layout
------
features/xdnetplay/pbr/ holds the format layer, and it deliberately references
nothing from Android: PbrBytes, Gen4Glyphs, Gen4Growth, Gen4Text, Bk4Mon,
PbrSave, Gen4Data and Bk4Factory compile and run on a plain JVM. That is what
makes the round-trip proof below runnable rather than aspirational. The two
platform touch points are isolated in one-purpose files: Gen4DataAssets.kt
(reads the asset out of a Context) and PbrSaveFile.kt (resolves the NAND path).

Bk4Mon keeps the decrypted 136 bytes verbatim and edits them in place, so
every field it does not model -- contest stats, ribbons, met location, shiny
leaf -- survives a load/save cycle untouched.

Gen 4 data asset
----------------
assets/xdnetplay/gen4data.json is the Gen 4 counterpart of gen3data.json:
493 species with base stats, both abilities, gender ratio, growth rate and
types, plus 467 moves, 527 items, 123 abilities, 25 natures, and display-name
tables indexed by id.

It is generated, never hand-typed. tools/gen_gen4.py is committed alongside it
and documents the exact fetch; the inputs are PKHeX @ 757a297f1463:

  Resources/text/other/en/text_Species_en.txt
  Resources/text/other/en/text_Moves_en.txt
  Resources/text/items/text_Items_en.txt
  Resources/text/other/en/text_Abilities_en.txt
  Resources/text/other/en/text_Natures_en.txt
  Resources/byte/personal/personal_hgss   (PersonalInfo4, SIZE 0x2C)

The same script emits Gen4Glyphs.kt (the Gen 4 name glyph table, scanned out
of StringConverter4Util.cs) and Gen4Growth.kt (the six experience curves from
Experience.cs plus each species' curve index). Those two live in Kotlin rather
than in the JSON precisely so the format layer stays JSON-free. Re-running the
generator reproduces all three files byte-for-byte.

Gen 4 quirks worth naming, since both bit us: the Gen 4 type ids are not the
Gen 3 ones (there is no unused "???" slot at 9, so Fire and everything above
it shifts down by one), and PersonalInfo4 repeats ability 1 in the ability 2
field for single-ability species, so "is this the second ability" has to test
for a distinct value, not just equality.

Editing
-------
Import accepts a Showdown export or a pokepast.es link. ShowdownParser is
reused from the Gen 3 editor unchanged -- it parses input, not a save format,
so it was already generation-agnostic. Bk4Factory is the Gen 4 MonFactory:
it picks a PID that satisfies nature (PID % 25), ability slot (PID & 1),
gender (PID low byte vs the gender ratio) and shininess in that priority
order, relaxing shininess first if the combination is unsatisfiable, with a
bounded search.

The per-Pokemon edit dialog covers nickname, level, held item, ability,
nature, moves, EVs, IVs and shiny. The PID is only re-rolled when the
requested nature, ability slot or shininess actually disagrees with the one it
already encodes -- renaming a Pokemon must not silently change its identity.
EV/IV fields are pre-filled as full six-stat Showdown spreads and parsed back
through ShowdownParser, so a field left alone round-trips instead of resetting.

Writing
-------
Same discipline as the Gen 3 editor, one step stricter because there is more
to get wrong. Saving rebuilds the container, re-checksums only the live
partition (small block first, then the big block that covers it), re-encrypts,
then parses the result back from scratch and requires: the active partition
did not move, every checksum still verifies, and every Pokemon in all four
profiles is byte-identical to what was in memory. Only then is the previous
file copied to PbrSaveData.bak and the new one swapped in through a temp file.
Any failure leaves the NAND save untouched and says so. A save that fails to
parse is never written over.

Also honest in the UI about what is not proven: PBR's Battle Pass teams live
elsewhere in the save and are not touched here, and Dolphin writes the NAND
back on exit, so the game should be closed before saving.

The asset is force-added past Source/Android/.gitignore's "*/*/*/assets/"
rule, the same way EMERALD-2.sav, EMERALD-3.sav and gen3data.json already are.
